    "q-161767": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161767",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}3{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=3{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =3{e}^{({\\rho }^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =3{e}^{{\\rho }^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}3{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}3{e}^{{\\rho }^{3}}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{\\rho }^{2}{e}^{{\\rho }^{3}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }d\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{\\rho }^{2}{e}^{{\\rho }^{3}}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi }\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot [{e}^{{\\rho }^{3}}]_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =1}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & =-(-1-1)\\cdot (2\\pi -0)\\cdot ({e}^{1}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =4\\pi (e-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
